   {
    "title": "Precedent Transactions and the Football Field",
    "minutes": 8,
    "body": "The third valuation method looks at what buyers actually paid to acquire similar companies. Then the **football field** chart puts every method side by side so you can see the overall picture.\n\n## Precedent transactions\n\nPrecedent transaction analysis (\"precedents\" or \"deal comps\") works like trading comps, but the multiples come from completed acquisitions instead of current share prices. For each deal you take the price paid for the whole target (its EV) and divide by the target's LTM revenue or EBITDA at the time.\n\nPrecedents usually produce **higher** values than trading comps because buyers pay a **control premium**. Control lets a buyer set strategy, cut costs and capture synergies, and it pays extra for that.\n\n## Strengths and limits\n\n| Strengths | Limits |\n|---|---|\n| Real prices paid for whole companies | Deals can be years old, from different markets |\n| Capture the control premium | Few deals, so small samples |\n| Directly relevant if a sale is possible | Deal terms and synergies differ |\n| | Private deals often disclose limited data |\n\nBecause of these limits, analysts use precedents mainly when a sale or acquisition is realistic, and give them less weight for a standalone valuation.\n\n## Worked example: Cloudline's precedents\n\nFour fictional acquisitions of B2B SaaS companies. Figures in $ millions, measured at the time of each deal.\n\n| Target | Year | EV paid | LTM revenue | LTM EBITDA | EV / Revenue | EV / EBITDA |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Lumora Software | 2024 | 640 | 160 | 30 | 4.00x | 21.33x |\n| Vellum Works | 2025 | 1,290 | 300 | 54 | 4.30x | 23.89x |\n| Ardent Ledger | 2025 | 940 | 200 | 40 | 4.70x | 23.50x |\n| Northvale Cloud | 2026 | 1,720 | 400 | 80 | 4.30x | 21.50x |\n\nSummary (QUARTILE.INC and MEDIAN, rounded to one decimal) and the implied value for Cloudline:\n\n| Statistic | EV / EBITDA | Implied EV | Per share | EV / Revenue | Per share |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 25th percentile | 21.5x | 1,161.0 | $26.03 | 4.2x | $28.50 |\n| Median | 22.5x | 1,215.0 | $27.38 | 4.3x | $29.25 |\n| 75th percentile | 23.6x | 1,274.4 | $28.86 | 4.4x | $30.00 |\n\nExample, median EV/EBITDA: 22.5 x 54.0 = 1,215.0 EV; 1,215.0 - 120.0 = 1,095.0 equity; 1,095.0 / 40.0 = $27.38.\n\nThe median implies $27.38 per share, about 37% above Cloudline's $20.00 share price. That gap is roughly the control premium a buyer might pay, not evidence that the stock is cheap today.\n\n## The football field chart\n\nA **football field** is a horizontal bar chart. Each bar shows the per-share value range from one method. The current share price is usually drawn as a vertical line.\n\nHere is Cloudline's summary table, in dollars per share:\n\n| Method | Range used | Low | High |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 52-week trading range | Market low to high (example) | 16.00 | 23.00 |\n| Comps: EV / Revenue | 25th to 75th percentile | 20.25 | 23.25 |\n| Comps: EV / EBITDA | 25th to 75th percentile | 21.17 | 26.57 |\n| Comps: P / E | 25th to 75th percentile | 18.76 | 22.35 |\n| Precedents: EV / EBITDA | 25th to 75th percentile | 26.03 | 28.86 |\n| DCF | WACC 9.5% to 10.5%, g 2.5% to 3.5% | 18.11 | 24.75 |\n\nAnd a text sketch of the chart (each # is about $0.50; current price $20.00):\n\n```text\n                        $14     $18     $22     $26     $30\n                        |-------|-------|-------|-------|\n52-week trading range       ###############  16.00-23.00\nComps: EV/Revenue                    ######  20.25-23.25\nComps: EV/EBITDA                      ############  21.17-26.57\nComps: P/E                        #######  18.76-22.35\nPrecedents: EV/EBITDA                           ######  26.03-28.86\nDCF (sensitivity)               ##############  18.11-24.75\n```\n\n## Reading the football field\n\n- **Look for overlap.** The DCF and all three trading comps bars overlap between about $21 and $22, and most of them cover roughly $20 to $24.\n- **Explain the outliers.** Precedents sit higher because of the control premium. That bar matters if someone might buy Cloudline.\n- **Place the current price.** At $20.00, Cloudline trades at or below the low end of the EV-based comps ranges and slightly below the DCF base case of $20.96.\n- **Order the bars with intent.** Many teams put market references (52-week range) first, then relative methods, then the DCF.\n\nFor a standalone view, a reasonable conclusion is a fair value range of about **$20 to $24 per share**. In a sale, the precedents suggest a buyer might pay around $26 to $29.\n\n> A football field shows each method as a range on one per-share scale, so you can see where the methods agree and explain where they do not.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- The football field is often the single slide senior bankers, managers or committees look at first.\n- Precedents set expectations for what a buyer might pay, which matters in M&A, fairness opinions and corporate development work.\n- Explaining why bars differ shows you understand the methods, not just the arithmetic.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Using precedent multiples to argue a stock is undervalued, forgetting they include a control premium.\n- Relying on old deals from a very different market without noting it.\n- Mixing EV and per-share values on the same chart.\n- Making the bars as wide as possible (for example the full min to max) so the chart says nothing.\n- Leaving out the current share price line, which is the reference point readers need.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Precedents value a company from prices paid in past acquisitions and usually include a control premium.\n- Cloudline's precedents imply $26.03 to $28.86 per share on EV/EBITDA.\n- The football field puts all methods on one per-share scale.\n- Cloudline's methods cluster around $20 to $24 per share on a standalone basis."
   },
   {
    "title": "Common Mistakes and Presenting a Range",
    "minutes": 8,
    "body": "You can now build a DCF, run comps and draw a football field. This final module covers the errors that most often break a valuation and how to present your answer as a defensible range.\n\n## The errors that matter most\n\nMost valuation mistakes fall into a few families. Each one has a quick check.\n\n| Error | Why it is wrong | Quick check |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cash flow and discount rate do not match | UFCF needs WACC; cash flow after interest needs the cost of equity | Is interest anywhere in the FCF build? |\n| Debt counted twice | Interest removed from FCF and net debt subtracted again | Same as above, plus the EV bridge |\n| EV bridge signs flipped | Debt is subtracted and cash added when going from EV to equity | Recompute equity = EV - debt + cash |\n| Terminal growth too high | Growth above long-run economic growth, or close to WACC | Compare g with WACC and with implied exit multiple |\n| Growth without reinvestment | Higher growth needs capex and working capital to support it | Capex vs D&A in the final year |\n| Multiple and metric mismatch | EV multiple on net income, or LTM multiple on NTM metric | Numerator and denominator on the same basis? |\n| Wrong share count | Basic shares overstate value per share | Diluted shares used? |\n| Unit errors | Thousands mixed with millions | Units labeled on every input |\n\n## Worked example: catching errors in a draft model\n\nA colleague sends you a draft Cloudline DCF showing **$17.08 per share**, well below your $20.96. You trace it and find they subtracted after-tax interest ($16.0M x (1 - 0.25) = $12.0M a year) from every UFCF, still discounted at WACC, and still subtracted net debt.\n\n```text\nCorrect:  UFCF at WACC, minus net debt          -> $20.96\nDraft:    (UFCF - 12.0) at WACC, minus net debt -> $17.08\nProblem:  debt is charged twice, once in the cash\n          flow and again in the bridge\n```\n\nA second draft shows **$26.96**. This time the cash flows are right, but the bridge adds net debt instead of subtracting it:\n\n```text\nWrong:    (958.30 + 120.00) / 40.0 = $26.96\nCorrect:  (958.30 - 120.00) / 40.0 = $20.96\n```\n\nBoth errors are easy to catch if you check the EV bridge and the FCF definition before you look at the answer.\n\n## A short review checklist\n\nRun these before sharing any valuation. Cloudline's results are shown for reference.\n\n| Check | Cloudline |\n|---|---|\n| UFCF excludes interest and debt flows | Yes |\n| WACC uses market-value weights | 80% equity / 20% debt |\n| g below WACC and modest | 3.0% vs 10.0% |\n| TV share of EV | 77.7% |\n| Implied exit multiple vs mature peers | 8.7x EBITDA |\n| DCF EV vs trading comps | DCF 17.7x LTM EBITDA vs peer median 18.8x |\n| EV bridge | 958.30 - 200.00 + 80.00 = 838.30 |\n| Diluted shares | 40.0M |\n\n## Presenting a valuation as a range\n\nA single number suggests more precision than any model has. Present a **range**, a **base case** and the **drivers** behind both.\n\n1. Show each method's range on the football field.\n2. Decide how much weight each method deserves for this purpose (standalone investment vs possible sale).\n3. State a fair value range where the most relevant methods overlap.\n4. Give a base case inside that range and say which inputs it depends on most.\n5. Say what would change your view, in measurable terms.\n\n## Worked example: Cloudline valuation summary\n\nHere is how the work from this course could be summarized for a portfolio manager. All figures come from earlier modules.\n\n```text\nCloudline Software: valuation summary (illustrative)\nFair value range (standalone):   $20 to $24 per share\nDCF base case:                   $20.96  (WACC 10.0%, g 3.0%)\nCurrent share price:             $20.00\nSupporting evidence:\n  DCF sensitivity (inner range)  $18.11 to $24.75\n  Comps EV/EBITDA, 25th-75th     $21.17 to $26.57\n  Comps EV/Revenue, 25th-75th    $20.25 to $23.25\n  Comps P/E, 25th-75th           $18.76 to $22.35\nSale scenario (precedents):      $26.03 to $28.86\nKey drivers: WACC and long-run growth (77.7% of EV\n  is terminal value); EBIT margin reaching 20% by FY2031.\nWhat would change the view: margins stalling near 16%,\n  or growth slowing faster than the forecast.\nConclusion: modestly undervalued to fairly valued on a\n  standalone basis; meaningful upside only in a sale.\n```\n\nNotice the summary avoids a single precise target. It gives the reader a range, the base case, the evidence and the key risk, which is what they need to make a decision.\n\n> Present a valuation as a range with a clear base case and the two or three assumptions that drive it, never as a single precise number.\n\n## Why this matters\n\n- Reviewers trust analysts who catch their own errors before the model leaves their desk.\n- Decision makers act on ranges and drivers; a precise point estimate invites false confidence.\n- Being able to say \"what would have to be true\" for a higher or lower value is a core financial analyst skill.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n- Reporting a value to the cent as if it were certain, with no range.\n- Averaging every method equally, including precedents in a standalone valuation without comment.\n- Hiding the assumptions that drive most of the value, such as terminal growth.\n- Presenting a range so wide that it cannot support any decision.\n- Skipping the review checklist because the answer \"looks reasonable\".\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n- Most valuation errors come from mismatched cash flows and rates, bridge signs, aggressive terminal values and inconsistent multiples.\n- Check the FCF definition and the EV bridge before you look at the answer.\n- Present a range, a base case and the key drivers, plus what would change your view.\n- Cloudline's standalone fair value is about $20 to $24 per share, with a $20.96 DCF base case."
   }
